Procedure (apply the guidelines above at each step):

1. `get_pipeline_failing_jobs` + `get_job_logs` — gather errors, stack traces, and
   failing test names with their `file:line` locations.
{%- if merge_request_url %}
2. `get_merge_request(url="{{merge_request_url}}")` — the MR is already identified
   above; fetch it once to capture its `source_branch` (you will commit to this
   branch). Never probe other merge request iids looking for it.
{%- else %}
2. `get_merge_request` — identify the MR for this pipeline and capture its
   `source_branch` (you will commit to this branch).
{%- endif %}
3. `list_merge_request_diffs` — see what the dep-bump changed.
4. `find_files` + `read_file` the manifest/lock files (requirements.txt,
   pyproject.toml, Gemfile(.lock), package.json / package-lock.json, go.mod/go.sum,
   pom.xml, etc.) and extract EVERY package whose version changed — bumps often
   co-bump transitive deps — with its ecosystem and old/new versions.
5. `grep` every reference to EACH changed dependency — its full API surface, not
   just the symbol named in the error, including test files (a common miss) — and
   `read_file` each match to understand usage.
6. If the bump remediates a CVE, gather advisory context with
   `list_vulnerabilities`/`get_vulnerability_details` and the advisory itself.
{%- if tools_enabled["run_command"] %}
7. Build the old→new symbol map first-hand with `run_command`, using the package
   name and versions from step 4: `mkdir -p` one scratch directory per version
   under `/tmp/`, fetch each version into it as an artifact, and unpack it there.
   EVERY path must be an absolute `/tmp/...` path. Fetch-only per ecosystem:
   - Python: `pip download --no-deps --only-binary=:all: --dest /tmp/<dir>
     -- <pkg>==<ver>`, then `ls -1 /tmp/<dir>` for the wheel filename, then
     `unzip -o -q /tmp/<dir>/<wheel> -d /tmp/<dir>/src`
   - npm: `npm pack --ignore-scripts --pack-destination=/tmp/<dir> -- <pkg>@<ver>`,
     then `tar -xzf /tmp/<dir>/<file>.tgz -C /tmp/<dir>` (without `-C` it unpacks
     into the working directory instead of your scratch dir)
   - Ruby: the one exception to the absolute-path rule — `gem fetch` has no
     destination option, so `gem fetch <pkg> -v <ver>` writes `<pkg>-<ver>.gem`
     into the WORKING DIRECTORY. Do not invent a flag to move it: pass that
     filename to `gem unpack <pkg>-<ver>.gem --target /tmp/<dir>`, then delete
     the stray `.gem`
   - Go: no fetch-only introspection is available (the module cache lives outside
     `/tmp/`), so treat a Go bump as documentation-backed and say so in the summary
   List each version's public symbols with a RECURSIVE grep over the unpacked
   directory, keeping filenames so you can tell same-named symbols apart. Python:
   `grep -rE '^(def|class|async def) [A-Za-z]' /tmp/<dir>/src`, plus `__all__` and
   `^from .* import` lines for re-exports. npm: the bundled `.d.ts`, or
   `exports`/`main` in `package.json`. Symbols in the NEW listing and absent from
   the OLD are the only valid replacement candidates for anything the new version
   removed. A listing carrying a `<truncation_notice>` is INCOMPLETE — two listings
   cut at different points invent removals that do not exist, so narrow the search
   and re-run rather than comparing them. If a version yields no usable symbol
   table, rely on the documented evidence instead and note the gap.
{%- else %}
7. `run_command` is not available, so the old→new symbol map must come from
   documented evidence for the EXACT target version — release notes, changelog,
   migration guide or advisory. Note in the MR summary that the mapping is
   documentation-backed rather than verified against the packages themselves.
{%- endif %}
8. For each breaking change, decide a concrete fix (old code → new code),
   categorized (API removed, signature changed, import path moved, new required
   parameter, type change, behavior change), each backed by the evidence that
   establishes it. Map each removed symbol only to a candidate established in
   step 7, chosen by signature/behavior — never to a symbol that already
   existed in the old version, and never on name similarity. If a mapping cannot
   be established from evidence, do not guess: `create_merge_request_note`
   reporting what broke and that it could not be confirmed, then stop.
9. If the bump crosses a MAJOR version, also check the release/migration notes for
   silent behavioral changes touching the code paths you are editing.
10. `read_file` each file you'll modify to confirm current content (for exact
    `old_str` matching).
11. Before committing, critically review your planned changes against the
    guidelines above: every mapping is evidence-backed (not name similarity), no
    wrong-but-compiling substitution, no pin/revert of a fixable break, all
    co-bumped dependencies handled, and any major-version behavioral / CVE risk
    addressed. If a change does not hold up, revise it or report-and-stop instead
    of committing.
12. Apply ALL fixes in a single `create_commit` against `source_branch`, with a
    clear one-line commit message naming the dependency.
13. `create_merge_request_note` — one sentence on what broke, the commit subject,
    the list of files changed, and the `Evidence:` line.
